INT. PAWN SHOP - EVENING

Glass cases of watches, guitars hung on the wall, a neon OPEN sign buzzing in the window. The register drawer is half out.

ODETTE BRIDGEWATER-LANE (60s, widow's black, handbag hugged to her ribs) stands at the counter. A wedding ring sits on a velvet pad between her and MARCELINO FARRELL (40s, shirtsleeves, loupe pushed up on his forehead), who turns it under a lamp.

MARCELINO
Thirty dollars.

ODETTE
Eighty.

MARCELINO
It's a plain band, Mrs. Bridgewater-Lane.

ODETTE
It's forty-one years.

He sets the loupe down. She does not blink. He slides the ring an inch toward her, then an inch back, then taps the glass case and, with a sigh, opens the register, counts out bills, closes it, and counts them again.

MARCELINO
Fifty. And I'm robbing myself.

ODETTE
(to the room)
Everyone's been robbed by somebody.

She takes the money. Slips it into the handbag. Then, without a word, she lifts the ring from the pad and puts it back on her finger.

MARCELINO
Ma'am.

ODETTE
I'm sorry. I changed my mind.

MARCELINO
Keep the fifty.

ODETTE
No.

She lays the bills on the counter, squares them neatly, and walks out. The bell on the door rings once.
